### 2A. Commercial Use Clarifications

The following principles resolve ambiguity between "Non-Commercial Purpose" and "Commercial Purpose":

* **(a) Hosted Services & API Use — paid subscription; no separate OCL Commercial License required for independent products.** Oxiverse operates a hosted search interface (free for personal and research use) and programmatic API access offered under a **paid subscription** (pricing published on our website; a free tier may be offered at Licensor's discretion). Independently developed applications, products, or services that consume Oxiverse's hosted APIs, platform interfaces, or services (Section 1(ii)) may do so for **any** purpose—commercial or non-commercial—**without a separate OCL Commercial License** from Licensor. You pay the applicable API subscription; beyond that, no commercial-licensing gate applies. Revenue generated by such an independent product belongs to that product and is not "revenue generated through Oxiverse services" for the purposes of this License. Such commercialization expressly includes direct and indirect monetization of the independent product—advertising, affiliate or referral links, sponsorships, and the like—without a Commercial License. Your independent product may be relicensed under any terms you choose (Section 1(ii)). Nothing in this subsection grants any right to the Software's source code; it concerns only consumption of the hosted service/API. The hosted service/API itself is a separate paid offering and is not licensed under OCL. For enhanced commercial use of the hosted services — such as white-label rights, redistribution of the hosted service, SLA, or custom integration — a separate paid Commercial License is available (Section 2A(d)).

* **(b) Self-Hosted Commercial Use — PAID Commercial License for closed-source; FREE if fully open-sourced.** Running, deploying, or operating the Oxiverse Software (the codebase comprising all Oxiverse services, including RAVANA and IntentForge) on your own infrastructure **for a commercial purpose** is governed by two paths:
  * **(b-i) Closed-Source Self-Hosting (PAID).** You may self-host the Software commercially in **closed-source / proprietary** form **only** under a paid Commercial License obtained from Licensor (Section 3). This is the commercial license Oxiverse sells. It permits you to operate the Oxiverse Software (any Oxiverse service) commercially without releasing your deployment's source.
  * **(b-ii) Full-Source Self-Hosting (FREE).** You may self-host the Software commercially **without** a paid Commercial License **if** you release the **ENTIRE stack** of that deployment under OCL v1.x or an **OSI-approved open source license** (e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0, GPL) — with no proprietary, closed-source, premium-only, or open-core components (see Section 3A). This path is free and does not require a paid license. "ENTIRE stack" is precisely defined in Section 3A.
  * Self-hosting for Non-Commercial Purposes (Section 2) remains freely permitted regardless of (b-i)/(b-ii).

* **(c) Funds vs. Revenue.** "Funds" received by a project—including donations, grants, sponsorships, crowdfunding, and institutional or organizational funding—are treated as **Non-Commercial**, provided they are not consideration paid by users for access to the Oxiverse Software or its services. "Commercial Purpose" is not limited to direct charges: it includes **both direct and indirect revenue generated by offering the Oxiverse Software or its services to end users**, such as (i) access or subscription fees for a self-hosted or hosted Oxiverse instance; (ii) reselling Oxiverse as a service; (iii) advertising or sponsored placements served through or on the Oxiverse service; (iv) affiliate or referral links monetized via the Oxiverse service; or (v) any other monetization derived from end users of the Oxiverse Software or services. This definition applies to use of the Oxiverse Software itself and does not reach independent API/service-consumer products under Section 2A(a), which may commercialize—including via advertising or affiliate links—without a Commercial License. A self-hosted deployment that complies with Section 2A(b-ii) and Section 3A (full-OSS) may generate revenue freely.

* **(d) Commercial Licenses for Self-Hosting & Hosted-Service Use — AVAILABLE (paid).** Oxiverse sells **paid Commercial Licenses** for commercial use of its Software and services. The primary commercial license is the **closed-source self-hosting license** (Section 2A(b-i)): it grants the right to operate the Oxiverse Software (any Oxiverse service) commercially on your own infrastructure in closed/proprietary form. Paid Commercial Licenses may also cover enhanced hosted-service use — white-label rights, redistribution of the hosted service, SLA, priority support, and custom feature development/integration. A Commercial License governs your **use** of Oxiverse's offerings and your independent product; for the closed-source self-hosting license, it expressly permits operating the Software without open-sourcing your deployment. To inquire, contact **[likhith@oxiverse.com](mailto:likhith@oxiverse.com)**.

* **(d-i) Open-Source Projects — optional FREE written confirmation of compliance.** Open-source projects that wish to self-host commercially under the full-OSS terms of Section 2A(b-ii), or use the API (Section 2A(a)), may request a **FREE written confirmation** that their intended use complies with this License. To obtain it, the project must have **contacted Oxiverse** ([likhith@oxiverse.com](mailto:likhith@oxiverse.com)) and met the applicable conditions (notably, for self-hosting, demonstrating the ENTIRE stack is open-sourced under OCL with no open-washing per Section 3A). This confirmation **confers no rights beyond those already granted by OCL** and grants **no proprietary, closed-source, or self-hosting-exempt permissions**. It is documentation of compliance, not a license — the open-source self-hosting path is already free without it.

