### 3A. Open-Washing Prohibition

"Open-washing" means presenting a deployment as "open source" or "OSS" while withholding material parts of the software that constitute or are required to operate the service. Open-washing includes, without limitation:

**Definition — "ENTIRE stack."** For the full-source self-hosting path (Section 2A(b-ii)) and this open-washing prohibition, "ENTIRE stack" means **all software components authored by, derived from, or incorporating the Oxiverse Software that are necessary to operate the Oxiverse-based service** — including all Oxiverse services (such as RAVANA and IntentForge), your modifications, extensions, and any glue code required to make the service function.

It does **NOT** require you to open-source independent third-party components that are not derived from the Software, such as: operating systems; databases and datastores (e.g., PostgreSQL, Redis); web servers and reverse proxies (e.g., Nginx); hosting providers and infrastructure (e.g., AWS, Cloudflare); external APIs and services (e.g., OpenAI, Stripe, S3); and general-purpose libraries or frameworks merely used by — but not modified from — the Software. You may deploy the Oxiverse-based service alongside such components in their normal proprietary or separately-licensed form.

* (a) **Open-Core:** publishing some components as open source while keeping other components required to run or meaningfully use the service under proprietary or closed terms;
* (b) **Source-Available-but-Restricted:** releasing code that cannot be freely used, modified, and redistributed under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license (such as MIT, Apache 2.0, or GPL);
* (c) **Premium/Closed Tiers:** offering a "community" open edition alongside a proprietary paid edition that contains functionality necessary to operate the service;
* (d) **Hidden Proprietary Dependencies:** requiring proprietary binaries, keys, services, or APIs that are not themselves available under OCL to operate the deployment.

Any deployment offered under the self-hosted commercial permission of Section 2A(b) MUST be genuinely, completely open: the **complete corresponding source of the ENTIRE stack** — including all modifications to the Oxiverse Software, all Oxiverse services, and every component that constitutes the service you operate — must be available under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license. Failure to do so is open-washing, constitutes a material breach of this License, and automatically terminates the commercial permission (and this License) under Section 9.

