### Quick FAQ

**Q: Can I use OCL software for my student project or portfolio?**
Yes. That's a Non-Commercial Purpose. Just keep the license intact, share source, and uphold privacy-by-design.

**Q: Can I add analytics to a modified version?**
Not without explicit, opt-in user consent. It must also comply with the Privacy Requirement, including minimization, transparency, and user control.

**Q: What if I enhance privacy (for example, end-to-end encryption)?**
Encouraged. You can go beyond the baseline. Just document your changes and keep the license.

**Q: What's the difference between "modifying the core" and "building on top of services"?**
Modifying the core means changing the actual Oxiverse source code (forking, patching, extending components). Those changes must stay OCL. Building on top of services means creating your own independent application that uses Oxiverse's APIs or platform—your app is separate and can be relicensed however you choose.

**Q: If I build a commercial app that uses the Oxiverse API, does my app need a Commercial License or OCL?**
No. The Oxiverse API is a paid subscription service (with a free tier for personal/research use); you pay the subscription to use it. Building an independent product on top of the API does NOT require a separate OCL Commercial License, and your app is not a derivative work of the Oxiverse Software (Section 1(ii)). You may license your product under any terms you choose—including proprietary/closed-source, OSI open source (MIT, Apache 2.0, GPL), or OCL v1.x (Section 2A(a)).

**Q: Does receiving donations, grants, or sponsorships make my Oxiverse-based project "commercial"?**
No. Funds such as donations, grants, sponsorships, and crowdfunding are treated as Non-Commercial, provided they are not payment from users for access to the Oxiverse Software or its services (Section 2A(c)). "Commercial" is broader than selling access: it also covers indirect monetization of the Oxiverse service—such as ads, sponsored placements, or affiliate links served through it—not just direct fees.

**Q: Can I SELF-HOST an Oxiverse service (e.g. RAVANA or IntentForge) and sell access to it?**
Two ways: (1) **Closed-source** — buy a paid Commercial License from Oxiverse (Section 2A(b-i)); this is the commercial license we sell and it lets you operate the Oxiverse Software (any Oxiverse service) commercially without open-sourcing. (2) **Full-source** — release the ENTIRE stack under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license (no open-core, no open-washing) for free (Section 2A(b-ii) & 3A). If you don't want to pay and don't want to open-source, use the paid Oxiverse API subscription from your own independent app instead (Section 2A(a)).

**Q: If I buy a Commercial License from Oxiverse, does that let me self-host any Oxiverse service (e.g. RAVANA, IntentForge) closed-source?**
Yes. A paid Commercial License from Oxiverse is exactly what grants the right to self-host the Oxiverse Software (any Oxiverse service) commercially in closed-source / proprietary form (Section 2A(b-i)). That is the commercial license Oxiverse sells. It covers use of the Software on your own infrastructure; it also covers enhanced hosted-service use (white-label, redistribution, SLA, support) where applicable. A full-source self-hosting deployment under Section 2A(b-ii) needs no paid license.

**Q: What counts as "open-washing" and why is it prohibited?**
Open-washing is pretending to be open source while hiding parts. Examples: open-core (some parts open, key parts closed), source-available-but-restricted, premium closed tiers with needed functionality, or hidden proprietary dependencies. Any self-hosted commercial deployment under the FREE full-source path (Section 2A(b-ii)) must release the COMPLETE stack under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license. Open-washing is a material breach and terminates the license (Section 3A). Note: a paid closed-source self-hosting license (Section 2A(b-i)) is permitted and is not open-washing.

**Q: I run an open-source project and want to generate revenue through Oxiverse — what do I need?**
If you only consume the API: pay the subscription (a free tier covers personal/research use); no separate OCL commercial license is required (Section 2A(a)). If you self-host commercially: you can either (i) buy a paid Commercial License for closed-source operation (Section 2A(b-i)), or (ii) release the ENTIRE stack under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license (no open-washing) for free (Section 2A(b-ii) & 3A). For enhanced hosted-service commercial use (white-label, redistribution, SLA), a paid Commercial License is also available from likhith@oxiverse.com (Section 2A(d)); open-source projects may also request a FREE written confirmation of compliance.

**Q: If I fork Oxiverse and add features, what license applies to my fork?**
OCL v1.x. Modifications to the core codebase must remain under OCL (see Section 1(i)). You cannot relicense a fork under different terms.

**Q: If I contribute code to Oxiverse, can I later relicense my contribution?**
No. Contributions become part of the Software and are licensed under OCL v1.x (see Section 6). They may not be relicensed by you or others outside the terms of this License. Oxiverse may use your contribution in its offerings only under the conditions in Section 6 — it does not place contributed code into a closed-source deployment.

**Q: How do I get a Commercial License?**
Oxiverse sells paid Commercial Licenses. The primary one is the **closed-source self-hosting license** (Section 2A(b-i)) — it lets you self-host the Oxiverse Software (any Oxiverse service) commercially without open-sourcing (this is the paid product). Paid licenses also cover enhanced hosted-service use (white-label, redistribution, SLA, custom integration) — email **[likhith@oxiverse.com](mailto:likhith@oxiverse.com)** (Section 2A(d)). The alternative to paying: release the ENTIRE stack under OCL or an OSI-approved open source license (Section 2A(b-ii) & 3A), which is free. There is no free closed-source self-hosting license.
